// Documented open emitter defect (D13, found while writing docs/learn-silica.md §10
// (2026-09-19)). A record or tuple holding two generic collections, returned across a
// module boundary and passed back in, arrives corrupted.
//
// Original report: job_queue@empty_jobs() returns a (PriorityQueue, OrderedSet) tuple
// (and the { queue, seen } record form); main receives it, and job_queue@waiting(j0)
// reads a corrupted queue.heap.count (16384 instead of 0), then the next call faults with
// case_clause. Inside job_queue itself the same construction reads back correctly
// (dedup3's probe2 answers 0) -- the corruption needs the tuple/record to actually cross a
// unit boundary and come back as an argument.
//
// This trial reduces the two stdlib collection types (PriorityQueue over brodal_okasaki,
// OrderedSet over wbt_set) to a tuple of two wbt_set instances (one over int64, one over
// string) built by pair_sets@make_pair and read back by pair_sets@waiting -- the smallest
// stdlib unit found to still reproduce it. A from-scratch generic module written by hand
// with the same record shape and the same region/ref?/canonical_arena_lookup recipe as
// wbt_set@empty (fresh_lifetime + canonical_arena_lookup, root: :none, no alloc_rec), with
// no other stdlib code linked in, did NOT reproduce the corruption -- so this is not simply
// "any record with a region field breaks in a tuple of two"; it needs the real wbt_set unit
// (or something about its size/frame complexity), not just its field shape. It looks like a
// stack-frame-relative address escaping the callee's frame instead of being promoted into
// the region before the value crosses the module boundary (spec §12.1 Region-Based Memory
// Management; uniform boxed record layout header of
// compiler/src/emitter/apple_silicon_mac/terms/prims/prims_record.silica).
//
// Expected: "0 at start", exit 0 (the int64 set is empty). Observed: a runtime fault --
// "[silica] fault ... in wbt_set_size+0x58 addr=0x0000000000003fff ..." -- exit 70, wbt_set@size
// takes the wrong case arm on set.root because the tuple's first element is a dangling
// address instead of a real (possibly heap- or region-, but definitely not
// already-popped-frame-) address.
//
// No .ascomp until fixed.

use pair_sets;

fn main() -> int64 {
    sequence proc[device_io]
        p: (
            { root: ref?(L, normal, rec), compare_item: fn(int64, int64) -> :less | :equal | :greater, region: region(L, normal), specialization_key: int64, compare_item_ordering_bundle: int64 },
            { root: ref?(L, normal, rec), compare_item: fn(string, string) -> :less | :equal | :greater, region: region(L, normal), specialization_key: int64, compare_item_ordering_bundle: int64 }
        ) <- pair_sets@make_pair();
        _: atom <- print_int64(pair_sets@waiting(p));
        println(" at start")
    produces
        pure 0
    end
}
